{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChL8QE4fLSCV8wtxvkcnP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I haven't made the diagram yet. Graphviz (`dot`) isn't installed here, and my check for the `diagrams` Python package needed an approval that wasn't granted.\n\nThe plan is the `diagrams` library. It bundles the official AWS icons (EC2, RDS, S3, CloudFront) and draws the layout the way AWS reference architectures do. The flow would be:\n\n**Users → CloudFront → two EC2 app servers → RDS Postgres**, with the app servers also reading and writing the **S3 uploads bucket**. CloudFront would also serve static and upload content from S3.\n\nI can do it one of two ways:\n\n1. **`diagrams` + Graphviz.** I'd run `uv pip install diagrams` in a venv inside the project. Graphviz would need a system install (`sudo apt install graphviz`), which you'd have to approve or run yourself. The output is a PNG or SVG with the real icons.\n2. **No system install.** I'd install `diagrams` in a venv only to get its icon files, then hand-lay out an SVG that embeds them. I control the placement, but it takes more work and looks less automatic.\n\nI recommend option 1. Which do you want? A few details would also help:\n- Should the two EC2 servers sit in separate Availability Zones, inside a VPC or subnet boundary?\n- Is there a load balancer (ALB) between CloudFront and the servers, or does CloudFront go straight to them?\n- Do you want PNG, SVG, or both?\n\nI'll assume multi-AZ, no ALB, and PNG plus SVG, unless you say otherwise."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":278,"cache_read_input_tokens":21781,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":278},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management